00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
british prime minister boris johnson's  authority to fight his country's record  coronavirus infections has suffered a  serious blow johnson's conservative  party suffered a crushing defeat in a  central england by-election the liberal  democrat party won that seat that has  been held by the conservatives for more  than a century the defeat is being seen  as a protest vote against johnson's  handling of coronavirus restrictions and  allegations that members of his  government did not respect their own  rules the u.k which had largely dropped  rules and restrictions is now debating  the best way forward as the omicron  variant looks to take over  church services replaced by the military  who are helping to administer booster  shots across the uk  over a teens are being encouraged to get  their third shot as soon as possible in  the fight against the new omicron  variant  there's enough supply but there's a  shortage of space museums are turning  into pop-up vaccine centers to ease the  demand on hospitals  it made sense to get it done as soon as  possible  it's really important for me to feel  comfortable seeing my family at  christmas  coronavirus cases are doubling every two  days thanks to omicron it's now the  dominant variant in london  daily cases are the highest they've been  since the start of the pandemic  the uk's chief medical advisor warned  lawmakers that this is just the  beginning what's the current r value  where we're going over the next next  couple of weeks the rate of increase is  going to be i think fairly uh fairly  impressive it is sensible for people to  cut down on work or other  interactions of people including  potentially social ones which are less  important to them so that they they  reduce their chances of catching covid  and indeed reduce their chances of  passing it on  but prime minister boris johnson has  other ideas  we're not saying uh that we want to  cancel stuff we're not locking stuff  down and the fastest route back to  normality is to get boosted  johnson is relying instead on masks and  vaccine and testing mandates  at larger events like football matches  they still allow  spectators you just need proof of  vaccination or a negative test  we'd eventually have to watch it at home  and that's  maybe not as good as coming into the  ground  introducing even these light  restrictions was no easy feat what  stalinist minds thought  the conservative party faces an internal  power struggle  some of johnson's own colleagues are  rebelling against him his authority  weakened by scandal  is the party over for you now prime  minister  even before thursday's by-election  defeat johnson's political room to act  was shrinking potentially worsening the  virus impact  the uk hospitalizations are slowly  increasing the only good news is that so  far death rates are not  i'm joined now from london by dw  correspondent charlotte chelsea pill  charlotte normally a by-election like  this wouldn't make headlines here but  why is this particular loss for the  conservative significant  well this is absolutely stunning for the  conservative party the party of prime  minister boris johnson just to really  put this  into context for you abby this is the  first time in almost 200 years than a  party that a party other than the  conservatives have won this seat this is  a very rural area and interestingly here  this is a brexit voting area and they  have just decided uh that the party that  brought them brexit the conservative  party of boris johnson is no longer for  them absolutely stunning results there  they overturned a majority of  over 20 nearly 23 000 seats that that  conservative lawmaker had prior to this  by-election stunning results there now  one of the reasons that it's being  looked at so closely not just here in  the uk but internationally as well is  that this is being viewed by a lot of  people as a referendum on the prime  minister boris johnson he has suffered a  number of scandals over the last few  weeks and many people are framing this  as a judgment on those scandals from the  british public indeed that is exactly  what the lawmaker from the opposition  lib dem party said when she won that  by-election take a listen  tonight the people of north shropshire  have spoken on behalf of the british  people they've said loudly and clearly  boris johnson the party is over  your government run on lies in bluster  will be held accountable it will be  scrutinized it will be challenged  and it can and will be defeated  our country is crying out for leadership  mr johnson you are no leader  tonight the people of north rupture have  said enough is enough  they have said that you are unfit to  lead and that they want to change  so you can hear there she said the party  of boris johnson can and will be  defeated and exactly those comments  after this stunning result that will be  listened to by members of the  conservative party fearing that perhaps  boris johnson isn't the electoral  winning ticket they once thought he was  and charlotte if he's not the electoral  winning ticket that he they once thought  he was is this the moment now that boris  johnson loses the support from within  his own party  well there have been rumblings of that  over the past few weeks as i say because  of this number of scandals that have  engulfed the conservative party just a  list a few uh the member of parliament  who stepped down triggering this  by-election stepped down because he was  found guilty of breaking lobbying laws  there have also been these allegations  that you and i have talked about abby  about uh conservative officials partying  when there were covert restrictions in  place the prime minister himself has  been  wrapped up in one of those allegations  of a gathering during a lockdown on top  of that the prime minister has seen  the biggest rebellion this week of his  premiership so far a hundred of his own  mps voted against him when new  restrictions were brought in brought in  uh for fighting covid so as you can see  there real real intense pressure on the  prime minister at the moment and that is  only building now the governments will  say that they need to get on with the  job but a number of conservative mps  will be wondering is prime minister  boris johnson the man to do it charlotte  before i let you go what does this mean  now for the pandemic outlook in the uk  we know that the omicron variant is  spreading quickly and as you said his  own uh  his own backbenchers went against the  measures that have now been implemented  how can he move forward here  well as i say the pr the prime minister  and uh spokespeople for the prime  minister will say this is exactly why he  needs to get on with the job because of  the pressure that the health service  particularly is under at the moment with  this surge in covert cases now as a  result of the omicron variant which is  now dominant here in london expected to  become dominant in the coming days uh  nationally there are fears that the  health service will be overwhelmed we're  hearing that there is going to be a  tidal wave of cases  the last two days have seen  record-breaking numbers that's crucial  context to this pressure that the prime  minister is facing dw correspondent  charlotte chelsea pill reporting from  london london thank you charlotte

